\section{Classifying preprint versions}
\label{sec:preprint-classification}

A published paper and its preprint are distinct records with distinct
DOIs, so when both appear in a result set (or when a lookup returns a
candidate preprint), Scholar must decide which record is the preprint.
We cannot ask the records themselves --- most APIs do not label
versions --- but two signals are reliable in practice:

\begin{description}
\item[DOI prefix] The large preprint servers each mint DOIs under their
  own registrant prefix, so the prefix alone identifies the server:
  arXiv uses [[10.48550/]], bioRxiv and medRxiv share [[10.1101/]],
  SSRN uses [[10.2139/]], Research Square [[10.21203/]],
  Preprints.org [[10.20944/]], and ChemRxiv [[10.26434/]].
\item[Venue name] Records without a DOI (or with a DOI from an
  institutional archive like HAL) still carry the server's display name
  as their venue, matching the provider labels in
  [[PREPRINT_SOURCES]] in [[providers.py]].
\end{description}

We keep these primitives here, rather than in [[providers.py]] or the
new [[preprint.py]], because both the dedup pass in [[scholar.py]] and
the fallback lookup in [[preprint.py]] need them, and [[utils.py]] is
the only module both may import without creating a cycle.

<<utils functions>>=
PREPRINT_DOI_PREFIXES = (
    "10.48550/",  # arXiv
    "10.1101/",  # bioRxiv and medRxiv (Cold Spring Harbor)
    "10.2139/",  # SSRN
    "10.21203/",  # Research Square
    "10.20944/",  # Preprints.org
    "10.26434/",  # ChemRxiv
)

PREPRINT_VENUE_NAMES = (
    "arxiv",
    "hal",
    "ssrn",
    "biorxiv",
    "medrxiv",
    "chemrxiv",
    "research square",
    "preprints.org",
    "iacr cryptology eprint archive",
)
<<utils functions>>=
def is_preprint_paper(doi: str | None, venue: str | None) -> bool:
    """Return whether a paper record looks like a preprint.

    Classifies by DOI registrant prefix (each preprint server mints
    DOIs under its own prefix) or, failing that, by the venue's
    display name matching a known preprint server.
    """
    if doi:
        normalized = doi.strip().lower()
        for prefix in ("https://doi.org/", "http://doi.org/", "doi:"):
            if normalized.startswith(prefix):
                normalized = normalized[len(prefix) :]
        if normalized.startswith(PREPRINT_DOI_PREFIXES):
            return True

    if venue:
        venue_normalized = venue.strip().lower()
        for name in PREPRINT_VENUE_NAMES:
            if name in venue_normalized:
                return True

    return False
@

The venue check is a substring match because providers decorate the
display names ([[arXiv (Cornell University)]] in OpenAlex, for
instance); an exact comparison would silently misclassify those.
The DOI check strips resolver prefixes the same way
[[Paper._normalize_doi]] does, so both spellings of the same DOI
classify identically.

Let's verify both signals and the negative case:

<<test functions>>=
class TestIsPreprintPaper:
    """Tests for preprint classification."""

    def test_arxiv_doi_prefix_is_preprint(self):
        assert is_preprint_paper("10.48550/arXiv.2401.12345", None)

    def test_doi_resolver_prefix_is_stripped(self):
        assert is_preprint_paper(
            "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.1234567", None
        )

    def test_decorated_venue_name_is_preprint(self):
        assert is_preprint_paper(None, "arXiv (Cornell University)")

    def test_journal_paper_is_not_preprint(self):
        assert not is_preprint_paper(
            "10.1145/3025453", "ACM Transactions on Something"
        )

    def test_no_doi_no_venue_is_not_preprint(self):
        assert not is_preprint_paper(None, None)
